eing like the  the flickering light that would come in  the dream sequence came from the above  as if it was the Sun rippling through  the water and then everything else in  there was kind of shaped by water all  the colors were in the aqua aqua blue  colors and our idea for that apartment  was that it was a Giles apartment was on  one side and Hurst was in the other but  it was split at one time rudely in the  in the 20s so there's sort of a deco Art  Deco detailed wall that went between but  on her side was the remnant Victorian  wallpaper from the building history  which was 1890s and then in that  wallpaper you know I had Shane sourced  had some beautiful wallpaper hand  printed wallpaper that were in the shape  of woodblock scales you know seems like  fish scales as a detail that you saw it  kind of at eye level and then everything  else was kind of you know quite brutally  aged and worn down from water leaking in  the ceiling and everything like that and  then opposite Jazz's apartment which is  very warm colours it was in sort of the  golds and mustards and and it was lit  warmly as well it meant to be a quite  different so you went from her cool blue  aqua tones across the hall into his warm  and his warm lit place to Dan lit his  side with sort of this Golden Hour kind  of lighting and on Eliza side it was  like always a steel blue kind of color  so it was like a real contrast but the  idea was that you know at some point  there was a grand room that got cut in  half she ended up in this rundown half  and he ended up in there really crowded  half and and he had a lot of he had more  means he had a bigger he must have come  from a house you know that was career as  an illustrator she had very little means  there was no art on the walls nothing  like that and and Guillermo said from  the beginning that the walls itself  would be like a piece of art so this is  where the heavy heavy aged patinated  walls that's where that derived from  he'd given me a photograph that he had  found online from a Indian photography  exhibit it was a real indigo blue wall  with heavy aging with distress on the  walls from humidity and he said I want  one of the walls to be like that and I  thought about it for a long time and  eventually I realized you know I can  use inspiration for anything to make  that whatever the texture and pattern on  the wall was and I chose I thought of  shapes of water literally shapes of  water in arts and I chose Hakka size  famous woodblock printing over the Great  Wave of Kenalog on so we superimpose  that in a drawing on one of the main  walls that we picked as our art wall and  then our scenic artist Matthew Lam Erick  painted it all in plaster very very  obvious like gimr walked in it goes oh  my god Paul it's like so literal I said  don't don't don't worry we're gonna  knock it way back and when we knocked it  way back essentially the mood was you  know there was this crashing wave that  went over top the entry door so anyone  coming into her world was just about to  be engulfed by water you know so that  was just these little subtle details in  that apartment that no one's ever gonna  know it without you listening to my  story but it's just we had to create a  little history for ourselves and it's  just some subliminal stuff I mean Jer mo  himself in previous movies that I've not  worked on he's done he's put little  subtle details in wallpaper patterns you  don't really see so I thought I'd take  take that idea what I understood that he  done it before and develop it somewhat  for this one so that's one world and  then we contrast that that world that  was meant to be romantic an old old  world is a you know curvaceous furniture  in her apartment we wanted to contrast  that with the the lab where she works  and the lab is kind of in for her it's a  bit of an oppressive place and I chose  again when I chose to use brutalist  concrete architecture style which was  you know I'm an architect when I was  pre